Often, yes—but not uniformly.

A lot of belief formation is incentive-sensitive. People don’t only ask, “Is this true?” They also ask, often unconsciously:

What happens to me if I believe this?
Does it protect my identity?
Does my group reward it?
Does it preserve status, hope, money, innocence, power, belonging?

That is motivated reasoning.

So a useful model is:

claim → perceived consequence of believing it → emotional/social payoff → acceptance threshold shifts

Two people can see the same evidence and weight it differently because the cost of the conclusion is different for each of them.

That does not mean truth is irrelevant. It means truth competes with utility.

And this is exactly why the Meaning Bank idea needs more than definitions. It needs to preserve:

what was claimed
what evidence supported it
who benefited from a particular interpretation
which alternatives were suppressed
what consequences followed from adopting each meaning

Because semantics can be bent by incentives long before anyone consciously decides to lie.

A sharper line might be:

People often believe what lets them keep being who they already think they are.

Then reality eventually charges interest.

That is where the Reality Key matters: separate what is useful to believe from what continues surviving contact with the world.

Best case, you can align the two:

make truth beneficial enough that people no longer need the lie.